=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The iptables FORWARD chain on router6, specifically three DROP rules applied to the `eth2` interface (`r6_r3` link — the only L3 path between router6 and router3).

**Mechanism:** Router6 sits between mswitch2 (and its attached subnets: 172.18.50.x laptops, 172.18.51.x desktops) and the rest of the routed topology (router3 → router4 → router2 → router1 → router0 → mswitch1). All forwarded packets that *enter* router6 via eth2 from router3 are matched by DROP rules (`-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP`, `-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP`, `-A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP`). The catch-all rule already shows 24 dropped packets — exactly the ICMP probes attempted during this session. The sole exception is a narrow ACCEPT rule for TCP port 80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1, which has never matched.

**Localization:** router6, iptables FORWARD chain, input interface eth2 (`r6_r3`). Outbound traffic from mswitch2 side exits eth2 normally; it is the **return traffic** (replies entering router6 from router3 via eth2) that is silently dropped, making all cross-topology sessions appear bidirectionally dead to the initiator.

**Root cause:** router6's iptables FORWARD chain has a blanket DROP policy on its eth2 interface, blocking all traffic arriving from router3 and breaking every path between mswitch1-side hosts (desktops/laptops on 172.16.x.x) and mswitch2-side hosts (desktops pc15–pc18 on 172.18.51.x and laptops laptop4/laptop5 on 172.18.50.x).

**Fix:** Remove the three DROP rules from router6's FORWARD chain on eth2: `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP`, `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP`, `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP`. Also remove or correct the startup script that reinstalls these rules on reboot.
=== END ===